// Dark Matter policy: what the analyzer detects and how findings become tiers and labels.
//
// Bump "version" whenever a change here can alter a result. Every report is stamped with
// this version and the SHA-256 of this file.
//
// The analyzer validates this file on load: every capability a rule or the analyzer can
// produce must appear in exactly one of "broadAccess" (in one of its groups), "labels",
// "fileAccess.kinds" or "informational".
{
  "version": "36",
  // v3: hardening phase 1 (late binding, Marshal, more deserializers, archive/XML/FileSystemInfo
  //     file labels, certificate store, services/WMI, temp folder counts as outside).
  // v4: hardening phase 2 (classification inversion): a framework member no rule matches is
  //     Full access (unclassified-reference); a call into an assembly that is not the mod, a
  //     trusted library, the game or the framework is Full access (unanalyzed-call). New
  //     "safe" list, informational "reflection" capability, Path.GetTempFileName is a file write.
  // v5: hardening phase 3 (reflection provenance, in the analyzer): invoking, creating, reading or
  //     writing a member or type that is not traced to a name is unresolved reflection, including
  //     through fields (resolved from every writer), Harmony "Type:Member" strings, patch methods
  //     and Traverse, Delegate.CreateDelegate by name, typeof(T) of a generic parameter, and
  //     bodiless extern methods (UnsafeAccessor). Finding text reworded.
  // v6: hardening phase 4, first half (close the boundary): a call into a trusted library applies
  //     that member's library profile (`profile --library`), Full-access capabilities and findings
  //     included; a trusted library without a profile for every trusted version is not analyzed.
  //     The game-API map keeps Full-access capabilities and covers non-public members. Curated
  //     exemptions in "game.exempt" and "libraries.exempt".
  // v7: hardening phase 4, second half: manifest resources that hold a program, library or archive
  //     (by leading bytes, a PE header anywhere, or a bundler's file name) are Full access
  //     (embedded-binary), as are linked resources. [HarmonyPatch] and [HarmonyDelegate] targets
  //     are decoded: a framework target gets its type's capabilities as reflection does, a game
  //     target its Full-access game-API capabilities, a trusted-library target its profile; a
  //     Harmony attribute type of the mod is untraced reflection, as are a TargetMethod result
  //     that is not traced, every TargetMethods result and an untraced Harmony.Patch original.
  //     Writes into the mod's or the game's folders get "Changes files in its own or the game's
  //     folders"; with a network connection (or with a write outside the game, which may be the
  //     same folders) they are Full access (fullAccessCombinations). A file method taken as a
  //     delegate is not path-traced. Trusted-library attribute constructors apply their profile.
  //     Harmony's attribute constructors are exempt like its patching API.
  // v8: review fixes. Reflection on a resolved type and Harmony patch targets are decided by where
  //     the type lives (game-API map, library profile, unanalyzed, unclassified framework member),
  //     including the assembly a type name names. A shipped file named like the framework, the game
  //     or a trusted library is Full access (name-collision). Reads through an address that is not a
  //     managed reference, initobj/cpobj/mkrefany, and by-reference arguments, locals and returns
  //     that are not managed references are raw memory. [HarmonyPatch] attributes inherited from a
  //     base class are merged. File paths are traced per operation (path atoms through arguments,
  //     fields and results; rooted literals are absolute paths) instead of over a neighbourhood
  //     ("traceDepth" removed). New rules: Vector Load/StoreUnsafe and aligned loads and stores,
  //     XStreamingElement.Save(path), ZipFile.OpenAsync, XML loads by URL ("fileAccess.urls":
  //     network unless the path is traced to a file), certificate
  //     files, the certificate store, chain building, persisted keys, MetadataUpdater.ApplyUpdate.
  //     Harmony's AccessTools exemption names the members modelled at the mod (MakeDeepCopy and
  //     GetDefaultValue are now sinks). Game profiles list the game's types (format 3).
  // v9: four tiers. New tier "unverified" between Known access and Full access, for blind spots
  //     bounded to the game's or the mod's own code and memory ("unverified" section): raw memory,
  //     unclassified framework members and calls into code that was not analyzed move there from
  //     "fullAccess". A mod is Full access if any Full-access reason or combination applies, else
  //     Unverified if any Unverified reason applies. What game and library members pass on through
  //     overrides and patches is still every capability that raises the tier. Detection unchanged.
  // v10: reflection precision and split (R3). Reflection whose target is known only up to a game or
  //     mod type and the types derived from it (obj.GetType() on an object of a declared game or mod
  //     type), or to any type of a game or mod assembly (typeof(X).Assembly.GetType of a name built at
  //     run time), or whose member came from a lookup on such a type and was then passed to code the
  //     check does not follow, is Unverified ("bounded-reflection"); everything else the check cannot
  //     trace stays Full access. Traced now: elements of member arrays and lists from GetMethods,
  //     GetProperties, AccessTools.GetDeclared*, ... (ldelem, foreach, LINQ First/Where/ToList, ...)
  //     until something untraced is put into them; Type and member parameters (from every call site,
  //     unless the method can be called from outside the mod) and return values (from every ret);
  //     closure fields (a public field of a private type is not assignable from outside);
  //     TargetMethods iterators (yield return) and returned arrays or lists. Expression factories
  //     (Call, Field, Property, New, ...) are reflection sinks and are classified ("reflection");
  //     compiling a tree built only from traced members is not code generation. Expression.Dynamic
  //     is late binding.
  // v11: game interaction and over-matching (R4). Harmony transpilers (CodeInstruction, CodeMatch,
  //     CodeMatcher, Transpilers, an ILGenerator's DefineLabel and DeclareLocal) are "code-rewrite",
  //     Unverified: the instructions Harmony compiles are the game method's own and ones whose
  //     members are traced (an instruction's operand is a reflection sink, and writing an
  //     instruction's fields by reflection is unresolved), and OpCodes.Calli is native code; OpCode,
  //     Label and LocalBuilder values are safe. Real code generation (Emit, DynamicMethod,
  //     ILGenerator.Emit, ...) is unchanged. A transpiler on a game member that an exemption names
  //     gets the exempted capability back. A Harmony patch whose patch methods can only observe (a
  //     void prefix, postfix or finalizer whose parameters are strings, numbers or __state) passes on
  //     nothing of a game or trusted-library target. Process.Start of a folder the method has just
  //     created with Directory.CreateDirectory, traced to the mod's or the game's folders, is
  //     "shell-open-folder" (a label). IPAddress and IPEndPoint are values (safe); NetworkInformation
  //     is "user-info", except Ping (network). A plural member lookup whose elements are only taken
  //     through LINQ filters that require a constant name reaches only the members of that name.
  //     XmlSerializer's constructors for one type are "xml-serialization" (Unverified, instead of
  //     unclassified); Serialize and Deserialize are safe; an XmlSerializerAssembly attribute is
  //     dynamic loading. An XML load whose
  //     path starts with a traced folder (an untraced part only appended) is a file read, not a
  //     possible web address. ELF and Mach-O files are native binaries, not unreadable files.
  // v12: review fixes (R6). A Harmony transpiler is Unverified only on the game's code: a transpiler
  //     whose target is the framework, a trusted library, the mod's own code, code that was not
  //     analyzed, or a target the check cannot place is Full access ("rewrite-outside-game", a reason
  //     of its own rather than "code-generation": the instructions are still traced, but the code
  //     they rewrite is not bounded to the game). The mod's own code counts as outside because the
  //     analysis of its IL, including what is proved on it (a folder it opens, a constant name),
  //     holds only as written. Targets: [HarmonyPatch] targets of a class with a transpiler,
  //     TargetMethod(s) results, Harmony.Patch with a transpiler, reverse patches (when the mod uses
  //     the transpiler helpers, as the stand-in can carry one), and PatchProcessor.AddTranspiler
  //     (whose original the check does not link: always outside). This also keeps trusted-library
  //     exemptions sound: a rewritten library member is Full access. A code-rewriting mod also gets
  //     "process-start" back on a game member it reaches or patches whose game-API map entry opens a
  //     fixed web address or a folder (shell-open, shell-open-folder), as for exempt game members:
  //     the instructions that proof is about can change. XmlSerializer for a type the check did not
  //     trace stays Unverified, as an explicit exception to the placement principle (the XML cannot
  //     choose the type). Framework semantics the analyzer
  //     models by name (reflection lookups and sinks, collections and LINQ, name filters, path
  //     combiners and sources, interpolated strings, ProcessStartInfo, Directory.CreateDirectory for
  //     folder opens, clean expression trees, string.Empty, inert patch parameter types) apply only to
  //     members that live in the shared framework, game path sources only to members of the game's
  //     assemblies, and Harmony's only to members of 0Harmony; a type named like one elsewhere is
  //     ordinary code. Harmony's patching API is exempt from "rewrite-outside-game" (it adds the
  //     transpilers it is given; what they rewrite is placed at the mod).
  // v13: three player-facing tiers. "content-only", "known-access", "unverified" and "full-access"
  //     become "no-code", "limited-access" and "full-access", each with a name and a one-line
  //     summary. Unverified is no longer a tier of its own: its reasons are the "deep-game-access"
  //     group of Full access. The Full-access reasons are split into three groups, reported apart:
  //     "deep-game-access" (raw memory, bounded reflection, transpilers on the game's code,
  //     XmlSerializer), "system-access" (native code, code loaded or built at run time, programs,
  //     unsafe deserializers, late binding, untraced reflection, COM, native binaries, transpilers on
  //     other code, and the network combinations) and "not-fully-checked" (unreadable code, files
  //     and archives, files not analyzed, scripts, embedded binaries, name collisions, the resource
  //     combinations, and, moved from Unverified, unclassified framework members and calls into code
  //     that was not analyzed: gaps in what was checked, not something the mod does). Detection
  //     unchanged; the tier of a mod that was Unverified becomes Full access.
  // v14: every label has a stable "id", which results carry next to its text (reasons are keyed by
  //     their capability or finding as before). Results also carry every piece of evidence
  //     behind a label or reason, sorted, with its count, instead of the first five. Detection
  //     unchanged: every mod keeps its tier, labels and reasons.
  // v15: gap closing (G2a, tier gaps found by the G1a review). Any method Harmony would use as a
  //     transpiler (named Transpiler, [HarmonyTranspiler] or [HarmonyILManipulator], a signature
  //     taking or returning CodeInstruction, a transpiler given to Harmony.Patch, ReversePatch or
  //     PatchProcessor.AddTranspiler) is "code-rewrite", whether or not it uses a CodeInstruction
  //     member. A trusted-library member whose exemption says its arguments are checked at the mod's
  //     call ("atCall": Harmony's patching API, HarmonyMethod, the patch attributes, the modelled
  //     AccessTools members, Traverse, the operand setters) is "unresolved-reflection" when it is
  //     taken as a delegate or method handle, or reached by reflection or a patch, since those
  //     arguments are then not checked. An operand that is a string on one path and something else
  //     on another is not inert. FirstOrDefault/SingleOrDefault/LastOrDefault/DefaultIfEmpty with a
  //     default value can return that default. GetMember, GetMembers and DeclaredMembers also find
  //     nested types: a name they are narrowed to reaches every member of the nested type of that
  //     name. A Type or member array handed to a member outside the mod's own assembly that the
  //     walker does not model can hold anything afterwards (untraced, not bounded). String
  //     arguments to a method of the mod's own generic type are recorded, and a member-name
  //     parameter with a call the check saw but no recorded value can name anything. An
  //     XmlSerializer for a type the code does not fix there (typeof(T), a Type parameter, a run-time
  //     type) is "xml-serialization-open" (deep game access, as "xml-serialization"); in the game it
  //     passes on "xml-serialization-game" (deep game access) instead of nothing, which a mod's
  //     call keeps only where it can choose the type (a type argument that is not the game's, or a
  //     Type, object or generic argument); reflection and patches on such a game member keep it. Only
  //     the game member that makes the XmlSerializer has it, not the game code calling that member.
  //     Mach-O universal files in the 64-bit byte-swapped form are native files.
  // v16: gap closing (G2b, path-label gaps found by the G1a review). The game folder in Documents is
  //     only the Documents folder itself followed directly by "/My Games/Kitten Space Agency" (in
  //     that order and case, "gameFolder"), not any path with "My Games" or "Kitten Space Agency" in
  //     it; an environment variable is never the game folder. A path parameter of a method that code
  //     outside the mod can call (public, a Harmony patch method, attributed) is also untraced, next
  //     to what the mod's own calls pass, as for reflection. Interpolated strings: ToStringAndClear starts a new text (which can also
  //     follow the old one), what is appended after a branch can begin the text, a handler with a
  //     format provider or whose address is used for anything but its own members is untraced, and a
  //     value that is not a string, number, boolean, GUID or one of the mod's own enums (a char, a
  //     type with its own ToString, a custom format, an alignment on a path) is untraced; char's
  //     ToString is untraced. Parts joined end to end (string.Concat, interpolation) that can make a
  //     ".." segment together (a "/." next to "./", or next to a value the check cannot read as text)
  //     are untraced, and so are string edits that can make one (Substring, Remove, Insert, most
  //     Replace and Trim calls, ChangeExtension, GetFileNameWithoutExtension, compatibility Normalize).
  // v17: name gating (G4). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: what R6 still matched by name alone
  //     must also come from the framework (see AssemblyWalker.Models): a type found by name no longer
  //     resolves to a mod type of that name alone (the framework's, game's or a library's counts too);
  //     a mod assembly named "System..." is not the framework; root base types, iterator interfaces,
  //     InlineArray attributes, inline-array helper callees, enum bases, delegate constructors, the
  //     types an interpolated value is formatted as, and compiler attributes on a method must be the
  //     framework's.
  // v18: explicit-layout overlaps (H2). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a type of the mod with
  //     [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Explicit)] whose instance fields share bytes is "raw-memory" unless
  //     both fields of every overlapping pair are plain data (integers, floats, bools, chars, and the
  //     mod's own enums and structs of those, a few framework value types such as Vector4 and Guid).
  //     A reference, pointer, native integer, managed reference, generic or other assembly's value type
  //     over anything is a type confusion with no API call; a field the check cannot size reaches to the
  //     end of the type.
  // v19: lookups by name that find what the name does not spell (H3). Analyzer change only, no rule
  //     changed: a member name a lookup reaches matches ignoring case (BindingFlags.IgnoreCase, flags
  //     that are not a constant, the by-name Expression factories, CreateDelegate's ignoreCase), and a
  //     name ending in '*' as a prefix (GetMember), in the game-API map, library profiles, framework
  //     nested types and game exemptions. Delegate.CreateDelegate(Type, Type, string, ...) and
  //     Expression.Call(Type, string, ...), Field/Property(Expression, Type, string) are lookups by name
  //     on that type. Reflection on a type of the mod reaches the types nested in it. A type found by
  //     name ignoring case, or through a Type.GetType resolver, is unresolved reflection.
  //     [IgnoresAccessChecksTo] naming a framework assembly is "unclassified-reference" (non-public
  //     framework members are not classified).
  // v20: span and stackalloc accounting made stricter (H4). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a
  //     span constructed in place (call instance Span<T>::.ctor(void*, int)) is read from its pointer and
  //     length arguments, after the receiver, and is accounted for only on the address of a local of
  //     exactly that span type; a stackalloc size is a count only from mul.ovf/mul.ovf.un (plain mul can
  //     wrap), and only from a local whose address is never taken; in an explicit layout, a field of a
  //     value type of the mod encoded as a class is of unknown size. Also: a local whose address is taken
  //     anywhere in the method (a ref local can write it where the walker does not look) is trusted to
  //     hold only its declared type, not the type, member, string, path, buffer, start info or
  //     collection last stored to it (an enumerator only while every address of it goes straight to a
  //     call that takes it): a web address, a created folder or a harmless type replaced through a ref
  //     local was Limited access before. The same holds where such a local is known from every store
  //     to it (after a loop head), and a collection or start info stored to it is no longer followed.
  // v21: the game-API map tells "read, reaches nothing" from "not read" (H5). Analyzer change only, no
  //     rule changed: a game method whose signature or body cannot be decoded reaches unanalyzed-call
  //     (and so does every game method that can reach it), where before it was left out of the map
  //     and read as reaching nothing; a method whose body is not IL (mixed-mode native code) is
  //     native-code, and a game assembly with native or precompiled code gives every one of its methods
  //     native-code (the namespace exemptions below still apply). The game profile lists every method
  //     and field the game defines: a call into a game assembly to a member this build does not define
  //     (removed, renamed, forwarded elsewhere) is unanalyzed-call, not a call that reaches nothing.
  //     Profile format 4.
  // v22: inherited game members (H5 review). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a member
  //     reference or a lookup by name on a game type also finds what its base types define, as the
  //     runtime and reflection do. A member a game base type defines reaches that member's map entry
  //     (before: not analyzed for a reference, and nothing at all for reflection or a patch target found
  //     by name, where GetMethod or AccessTools.Method run the base type's member); one a framework
  //     base type defines is classified by the rules below as a direct reference to it; one neither
  //     has stays unanalyzed-call. Profile format 5: members by type, and each game type's base type.
  //     Also: a member reference that names one of the mod's (or a library's) own types for a member
  //     neither it nor its base types there define (hand-written metadata; the runtime runs what it
  //     inherits from another assembly, Process.Kill through a type deriving from Process) is
  //     unanalyzed-call; before, it read as the mod's own code and reached nothing.
  // v23: reflection precision (H7). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a method of the mod's own
  //     type counts as reached by reflection (any argument) only when a lookup on the type can return
  //     it (by its name, ignoring case, a name ending in '*' as a prefix, a property's or event's name
  //     for the methods the metadata gives it, a nested type found through its outer type), not for
  //     every lookup on the type; lookups by a name parameter and through traced Type fields are
  //     iterated together. A collection kept on the stack across a block start is followed on when
  //     every way in carries it there; one kept along a backward branch is not followed (before, a
  //     conditional backward branch kept it, Limited access). A branch into the middle of an
  //     instruction (or an exception region not on instructions) is code the check cannot read.
  //     Traverse.Create(object) on an object of a declared class or struct is that type (exactly, for
  //     a new or boxed one) or a type derived from it, as for GetType. InvokeMember("") (the default
  //     member) reaches any member.
  // v24: H7 review. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a lookup of a game or trusted-library type's
  //     property, event or indexer by a name that neither it nor any accessor name of it has (an indexer
  //     renamed with [IndexerName] found by AccessTools.Indexer, an explicit interface implementation,
  //     hand-written IL) reaches every member of the type; before, nothing. A Harmony getter or setter
  //     patch without a name patches the indexer's accessor, a finalizer patch Finalize, an operator patch
  //     the type's op_ methods (before: no target). A method of the mod's own taken as a method handle
  //     (ldtoken: an expression tree's call, compiled) is called with any arguments, as a delegate is; and
  //     one reflection can reach gets any path arguments, as one called from outside does (before, a
  //     private helper invoked by name kept only its direct calls' paths).
  // v25: H7b. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a lookup or a Harmony patch named through one of
  //     the mod's own types also reaches what the type inherits from outside the mod (a game base type
  //     through the game-API map, a trusted library's by its profile, a framework type by these rules
  //     for the names it has; before, nothing). A static method that implements a static abstract or
  //     static virtual interface member (called through a type parameter) is called with any arguments,
  //     as a virtual method is. GetType() of a value of a game (or any non-mod) declared type also
  //     reaches the mod's types that derive from it, by the lookup's names.
  // v26: H7b review. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a type nested in the looked-up type (the
  //     mod's or the game's; the check counts a nested type found from a type as that type) is also
  //     searched with what it inherits from outside the mod, and a mod type's own indexer no longer
  //     keeps an indexer lookup (found by its parameters, whatever it is called) from a base type's.
  // v27: H8, raw-memory precision. The unsafe compile flag (UnverifiableCode) alone is informational
  //     ("unsafe-code"): the runtime gives it no meaning, and every unsafe construct is found in the IL.
  //     Members that touch no memory are safe, matched by their full signature: Marshal.OffsetOf (Type,
  //     string and the generic form), Unsafe.SizeOf and IsNullRef, and the error-code helpers
  //     SetLastPInvokeError, SetLastSystemError, GetHRForLastWin32Error, GetPInvokeErrorMessage and the
  //     (int) forms of ThrowExceptionForHR and GetExceptionForHR (the IntPtr forms take an IErrorInfo
  //     pointer and stay raw memory). Analyzer: a constant 0 stored as a reference is null; a stackalloc
  //     of 1-byte elements with a variable count (no mul) into a span of that element is accounted
  //     for under the count rules (the same store of a local whose address is never taken).
  // v28: H8 review. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a stackalloc span's length must be the
  //     count's own value (a plain load of the same store, not the buffer, a product or a count kept in
  //     another local); a count or counted buffer stored to a local is only a value; and stackalloc in a
  //     method without localsinit ([SkipLocalsInit]) is raw memory (the buffer holds old stack data).
  // v29: H9, classification. GCHandle: Alloc, IsAllocated and comparisons are safe; FromIntPtr, the
  //     explicit conversions, ToIntPtr, Free and Target act on whatever handle address they are given
  //     (raw memory). System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshalling (the [LibraryImport] marshallers) is raw
  //     memory, its COM wrappers native code. The CPU feature checks (IsSupported) of the ISA classes the
  //     index uses are safe. TextFieldParser opening a path is a file read (the path is traced), the rest
  //     of it safe. XmlSerializer(Type, XmlAttributeOverrides) is "xml-serialization-open" (the types the
  //     overrides name are not followed), its event and attribute-collection types safe. DriveInfo's
  //     queries are user-info (renaming a volume stays unclassified). Process.GetCurrentProcess and
  //     StartTime are safe. New label capability "ipc" ("Communicates with other programs on your
  //     computer"): named Mutex, Semaphore and EventWaitHandle and their OpenExisting/TryOpenExisting; the
  //     unnamed forms and their instance members are safe. Analyzer: a type found by a constant name with
  //     no assembly part that nothing has, whose result is only tested for null, is not unanalyzed-call; a
  //     delegate constructed from a native int that no ldftn/ldvirtftn in the method produced is native
  //     code (it calls whatever address it is given); a call into an assembly named like the game's that
  //     this game build does not have is the new finding "missing-game-code" (not fully checked).
  // v30: H9 review. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: the null-test exception applies to lookups only
  //     (AccessTools.TypeByName, Assembly.GetType, Module.GetType), not to Assembly.CreateInstance, which
  //     runs a constructor before its result is tested; a type created by name (Assembly.CreateInstance,
  //     Activator/AppDomain.CreateInstance(assembly, type)) reaches its constructors, as
  //     Activator.CreateInstance(Type) does (before, a game or library type's constructor was not reached).
  // v31: H10b review. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: a member reference that names a type of the mod
  //     (or of a trusted library being profiled) is that code's own member only when the type or one of its
  //     base types there defines a member of that name with the same signature; the runtime matches name and
  //     signature, so a reference to Kill() through a type that derives from Process and declares only
  //     Kill(int) runs Process.Kill (unanalyzed-call; before, the name alone made it the mod's own code). An
  //     [IgnoresAccessChecksTo] name is parsed as an assembly display name, quotes included (a quoted
  //     framework assembly name was not recognized); a name that does not parse is unclassified-reference.
  // v32: H12. Analyzer change only, no rule changed: that signature is also matched when it names the same
  //     types through other tokens (a type reference into the module for its type definition, one framework
  //     facade's System.Exception for another's, as merged assemblies write them; before, only the same bytes
  //     matched, and such a mod's own member counted as unanalyzed-call). A type reference into the module is
  //     resolved part by part (a type named "A/B" is not B nested in A). A framework type no readable runtime
  //     file defines is taken to declare a member name looked up on it (environment only).
  // v33: B review (direct memory access). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: the cpblk and initblk
  //     opcodes are raw memory whatever their addresses are. Before, only their address operands were
  //     checked for being managed references; their byte-count operand was not, so a block copy or fill
  //     of a caller-chosen length through a managed address (a write past the destination's own size) was
  //     Limited access. The C# compiler never emits a bare cpblk/initblk (it routes through Unsafe/Buffer,
  //     caught by their own rules), so the two appear only in hand-written IL and always fail closed now.
  // v34: C review (memory-safe patterns). Analyzer change only, no rule changed: four shapes the runtime keeps
  //     memory-safe are no longer raw memory. C1: a span of enum elements over a stackalloc buffer (the enum's
  //     size is its one instance field's, read from the mod's or the framework's metadata). C2: MemoryMarshal
  //     AsBytes, Cast, Read, Write, TryRead and TryWrite with every type argument plain data (no reference,
  //     pointer or native integer, nested structs included). C3: explicit-layout unions over framework enums
  //     and plain framework structs, laid out from the shared framework's own metadata (a framework type that
  //     holds a reference stays unknown). C4: the stores of a stackalloc initializer of primitive or enum
  //     elements, in every shape the compiler emits (stind at constant offsets, initblk, cpblk from constant
  //     data by address or through RuntimeHelpers.CreateSpan): each store's offset and size, or the copy's
  //     length, is checked against the buffer's constant size; v33's cpblk/initblk rule stays for every other
  //     shape (it turned out the compiler does emit them, for initializers of three or more constants). C5: the
  //     game-API map records the game's [InlineArray] types (profile format 6), so a mod's compiler-generated
  //     inline-array helper over a game type is checked like one over its own. Game-API map format 6.
  // v35: D (typed reflection reads). Analyzer change only, no rule changed. A member found by a constant name on
  //     exactly a resolved type (typeof, a constant type name) carries its declared type when every member of
  //     that kind the name can find there and on the base types (in any casing) is declared as one class or
  //     struct of the mod's own assembly or of the game (game-API map format 7 records the declared type of
  //     every game member, and each game type's interfaces). D5: FieldInfo/PropertyInfo.GetValue through it is
  //     an object of that type or a derived one, as GetType() of a declared value already was (a property only
  //     when its getter returns its type). D6: FieldType, PropertyType and ReturnType are exactly that type.
  //     Both also through a member kept in a field, parameter or return value. D7: a Harmony prefix, postfix or
  //     finalizer's __instance declared as object is an instance of the patched type or a derived one, when the
  //     attributes name it by typeof and it alone declares the patched method, and the mod's own code neither
  //     calls the patch method, takes it as a delegate, reaches it by reflection (new HarmonyMethod(type, name)
  //     now counts as a lookup by that name) nor stores into the parameter. D9: a lookup on a game type known only
  //     up to its derived types also reaches the same members of the game's types deriving from it (by base type
  //     or interface). D10: a local whose address is taken somewhere in the method no longer keeps the last
  //     object stored into it, only its declared type (a store through a ref local could replace it). Results
  //     can only move from "Uses reflection the check cannot trace" to bounded reflection or to traced members,
  //     gain labels and reasons (D9) or move to untraced (D10).
  // v36: the top tier "Full access" is renamed "Broad access", with no change to what puts a mod in it or to
  //     its summary. The tier id "full-access" becomes "broad-access", the policy sections "fullAccess" and
  //     "fullAccessCombinations" become "broadAccess" and "broadAccessCombinations", and results carry
  //     "broadAccess" for their reason groups. The pipeline reads results from before v36 as the new names.

  // Tiers, lowest first. A mod is in the highest tier that applies: Broad access when any reason in
  // "broadAccess" or "broadAccessCombinations" applies, else Limited access when it ships code, else
  // No code. The name and summary are shown to the user.
  "tiers": {
    "no-code": { "name": "No code", "summary": "Only adds content. It can't run programs." },
    "limited-access": { "name": "Limited access", "summary": "It can only do what's listed." },
    "broad-access": { "name": "Broad access", "summary": "It can do anything a program on your computer can." }
  },

  // Capability rules. Each pattern is matched against a referenced member, written
  // "Namespace.Type::Member" (nested types as "Outer/Inner"):
  //   "Prefix"    the name starts with Prefix (a namespace, type or member prefix)
  //   "Exact$"    the name equals Exact
  //   "::Member"  the name contains "::Member", in any type
  // A "#N" suffix additionally requires the call to take exactly N parameters.
  "capabilities": {
    "network": [
      "System.Net.Http.", "System.Net.Sockets.", "System.Net.WebClient", "System.Net.WebRequest",
      "System.Net.HttpWebRequest", "System.Net.FtpWebRequest", "System.Net.Dns", "System.Net.WebSockets.",
      "System.Net.HttpListener", "System.Net.Mail.", "System.Net.Security.",
      "System.Net.Quic.", "System.IO.Pipes.", "System.Net.ServicePointManager",
      // NetworkInformation reads the computer's own network setup (user-info), except Ping, which sends packets
      "System.Net.NetworkInformation.Ping",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore.", "Grpc.",
      // building a certificate chain downloads missing intermediates and revocation lists
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Chain::Build"
    ],

    // File system. File.Open and FileStream can read or write, so they count as both.
    "file-write": [
      "System.IO.File::Write", "System.IO.File::Append", "System.IO.File::Create", "System.IO.File::Copy",
      "System.IO.File::Move", "System.IO.File::Replace", "System.IO.File::Open$", "System.IO.File::OpenWrite",
      "System.IO.File::OpenHandle", "System.IO.File::Set", "System.IO.File::Encrypt", "System.IO.File::Decrypt",
      "System.IO.StreamWriter::.ctor", "System.IO.FileStream::.ctor",
      "System.IO.FileInfo::Create", "System.IO.FileInfo::Open$", "System.IO.FileInfo::OpenWrite",
      "System.IO.FileInfo::AppendText", "System.IO.FileInfo::CopyTo", "System.IO.FileInfo::MoveTo",
      "System.IO.FileInfo::Replace", "System.IO.FileInfo::set_",
      "System.IO.Directory::Create", "System.IO.Directory::Move", "System.IO.Directory::Set",
      "System.IO.DirectoryInfo::Create", "System.IO.DirectoryInfo::MoveTo", "System.IO.DirectoryInfo::set_",
      "System.IO.RandomAccess::Write", "System.IO.MemoryMappedFiles.",
      "System.IO.FileSystemInfo::set_", "System.IO.FileSystemInfo::CreateAsSymbolicLink",
      // archives extracted or created on disk
      "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::ExtractToDirectory", "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::CreateFromDirectory",
      "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::Open$", "System.IO.Compression.ZipFileExtensions::ExtractToDirectory",
      "System.IO.Compression.ZipFileExtensions::ExtractToFile", "System.IO.Compression.ZipFileExtensions::CreateEntryFromFile",
      "System.Formats.Tar.TarFile::ExtractToDirectory", "System.Formats.Tar.TarFile::CreateFromDirectory",
      "System.Formats.Tar.TarEntry::ExtractToFile",
      // creates an empty file in the temp folder
      "System.IO.Path::GetTempFileName",
      // XML documents saved to a path
      "System.Xml.XmlDocument::Save(System.String)", "System.Xml.XmlWriter::Create(System.String,...)",
      "System.Xml.Linq.XDocument::Save(System.String,...)", "System.Xml.Linq.XElement::Save(System.String,...)",
      "System.Xml.Linq.XStreamingElement::Save(System.String,...)",
      // opens an archive on disk for update (OpenAsync is not matched by "Open$")
      "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::OpenAsync"
    ],
    "file-delete": [
      "System.IO.File::Delete", "System.IO.Directory::Delete", "System.IO.FileInfo::Delete", "System.IO.DirectoryInfo::Delete",
      "System.IO.FileSystemInfo::Delete"
    ],
    "file-read": [
      "System.IO.File::Read", "System.IO.File::Open", "System.IO.File::Exists", "System.IO.File::Get",
      "System.IO.StreamReader::.ctor", "System.IO.FileStream::.ctor", "System.IO.Directory::Get",
      "System.IO.Directory::Enumerate", "System.IO.Directory::Exists", "System.IO.DirectoryInfo::",
      "System.IO.FileInfo::", "System.IO.FileSystemInfo::", "System.IO.RandomAccess::Read",
      "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::ExtractToDirectory", "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::OpenRead", "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::Open$",
      "System.Formats.Tar.TarFile::ExtractToDirectory",
      // XML documents loaded from a path
      "System.Xml.XmlDocument::Load(System.String)", "System.Xml.XmlReader::Create(System.String,...)",
      "System.Xml.Linq.XDocument::Load(System.String,...)", "System.Xml.Linq.XElement::Load(System.String,...)",
      "System.IO.Compression.ZipFile::OpenAsync",
      // a CSV parser opening the file at a path (the Stream and TextReader forms read what the mod has)
      "Microsoft.VisualBasic.FileIO.TextFieldParser::.ctor(System.String,...)",
      // certificates and keys loaded from files
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate::.ctor(System.String,...)",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate::CreateFromCertFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate::CreateFromSignedFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate::Import(System.String,...)",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2::.ctor(System.String,...)",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2::Import(System.String,...)",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2::CreateFromPemFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2::CreateFromEncryptedPemFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2::GetCertContentType(System.String)",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2Collection::Import(System.String,...)",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2Collection::ImportFromPemFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509CertificateLoader::LoadCertificateFromFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509CertificateLoader::LoadPkcs12FromFile",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509CertificateLoader::LoadPkcs12CollectionFromFile"
    ],
    "file-watch": ["System.IO.FileSystemWatcher"],

    // Information about the user and the computer
    "user-info": [
      "System.Environment::get_UserName", "System.Environment::get_UserDomainName",
      "System.Environment::get_MachineName", "System.Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity",
      // the user's certificates and persisted keys
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Store::Open", "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Store::get_Certificates",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.CngKey::Open", "System.Security.Cryptography.CngKey::Exists",
      // the computer's network interfaces, addresses, connections, host and domain name
      "System.Net.NetworkInformation.",
      // the computer's drives, their names, labels, formats and sizes (renaming a volume is not listed: unclassified)
      "System.IO.DriveInfo::GetDrives", "System.IO.DriveInfo::.ctor", "System.IO.DriveInfo::get_", "System.IO.DriveInfo::ToString"
    ],
    "environment-read": [
      "System.Environment::GetEnvironmentVariable", "System.Environment::ExpandEnvironmentVariables"
    ],
    "process-list": ["System.Diagnostics.Process::GetProcess"],
    "registry-read": ["Microsoft.Win32.Registry"],

    // Persistent changes to the computer
    "registry-write": [
      "Microsoft.Win32.Registry::SetValue", "Microsoft.Win32.RegistryKey::SetValue",
      "Microsoft.Win32.RegistryKey::CreateSubKey", "Microsoft.Win32.RegistryKey::DeleteSubKey",
      "Microsoft.Win32.RegistryKey::DeleteValue", "Microsoft.Win32.RegistryKey::SetAccessControl"
    ],
    "environment-persist": ["System.Environment::SetEnvironmentVariable$#3"],
    "certificate-store": [
      "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Store::Add", "System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Store::Remove",
      // keys persisted in the user's or the computer's key store (a named CNG key, a CSP key container)
      "System.Security.Cryptography.CngKey::Create", "System.Security.Cryptography.CngKey::Delete", "System.Security.Cryptography.CngKey::SetProperty",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.CspParameters", "System.Security.Cryptography.RSACryptoServiceProvider::set_PersistKeyInCsp",
      "System.Security.Cryptography.DSACryptoServiceProvider::set_PersistKeyInCsp"
    ],
    "environment-process": ["System.Environment::SetEnvironmentVariable$#2"],

    // Other programs
    // Services and WMI can start programs.
    "process-start": ["System.Diagnostics.Process::Start", "System.ServiceProcess.", "System.Management."],
    "process-kill": ["System.Diagnostics.Process::Kill", "System.Diagnostics.Process::CloseMainWindow"],
    // Named kernel objects are shared with every program of the user (of the computer, with the Global prefix): a
    // signal to a cooperating program, or one that holds or sets another program's (a single-instance lock).
    // The unnamed forms are safe. Pipes are "network" (a named pipe can be on another computer).
    "ipc": [
      "System.Threading.Mutex::.ctor(System.Boolean,System.String,...)", "System.Threading.Mutex::.ctor(System.String,...)",
      "System.Threading.Mutex::OpenExisting", "System.Threading.Mutex::TryOpenExisting",
      "System.Threading.Semaphore::.ctor(System.Int32,System.Int32,System.String,...)",
      "System.Threading.Semaphore::OpenExisting", "System.Threading.Semaphore::TryOpenExisting",
      "System.Threading.EventWaitHandle::.ctor(System.Boolean,System.Threading.EventResetMode,System.String,...)",
      "System.Threading.EventWaitHandle::OpenExisting", "System.Threading.EventWaitHandle::TryOpenExisting"
    ],

    // Devices and the desktop
    "clipboard": ["::SetClipboardText", "::GetClipboardText"],
    "serial-port": ["System.IO.Ports."],

    // Code the check cannot follow
    "native-code": [
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.NativeLibrary::",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetDelegateForFunctionPointer",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetFunctionPointerForDelegate",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetActiveObject",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::BindToMoniker",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.ComWrappers",
      // the COM source generator's wrappers and marshallers (the rest of Marshalling is raw memory)
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshalling.StrategyBasedComWrappers", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshalling.ComObject",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshalling.ComInterfaceMarshaller", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshalling.UniqueComInterfaceMarshaller",
      "System.Runtime.Loader.AssemblyLoadContext::LoadUnmanagedDll",
      // an instruction that calls through a function pointer, for a transpiler or ILGenerator to emit
      "System.Reflection.Emit.OpCodes::Calli"
    ],
    "raw-memory": [
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::Copy", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::AllocHGlobal",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::Write", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::Read",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::PtrToStructure", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::StructureToPtr",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.NativeMemory::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe::",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.MemoryMarshal::",
      // every Marshal member not listed elsewhere; the safe list carves out the harmless ones
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::AddrOfPinnedObject",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.SafeBuffer",
      // a handle made from, or turned into, a native int, freed or dereferenced: GCHandle is a struct, so a
      // copy (or a forged one) can free or read a slot that belongs to another handle
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::FromIntPtr", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::op_Explicit",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::ToIntPtr", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::Free",
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::get_Target", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::set_Target",
      // the [LibraryImport] marshallers: copy data to and from unmanaged memory and free it
      "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshalling.",
      // vector loads and stores at an unchecked offset from a reference (and aligned ones, through a pointer)
      "System.Numerics.Vector::LoadUnsafe", "System.Numerics.Vector::StoreUnsafe", "System.Numerics.Vector::LoadAligned",
      "System.Numerics.Vector::StoreAligned", "System.Numerics.Vector2::LoadUnsafe", "System.Numerics.Vector2::LoadAligned",
      "System.Numerics.Vector3::LoadUnsafe", "System.Numerics.Vector3::LoadAligned", "System.Numerics.Vector4::LoadUnsafe",
      "System.Numerics.Vector4::LoadAligned"
    ],
    "dynamic-load": [
      "System.Reflection.Assembly::Load", "System.Reflection.Assembly::UnsafeLoadFrom",
      "System.Runtime.Loader.AssemblyLoadContext::LoadFrom", "System.AppDomain::Load",
      "System.AppDomain::ExecuteAssembly", "System.AppDomain::CreateInstanceFrom", "System.Activator::CreateInstanceFrom"
    ],
    "code-generation": [
      "System.Reflection.Emit.", "System.Linq.Expressions.LambdaExpression::Compile", "System.Linq.Expressions.LambdaExpression::CompileToMethod",
      "System.Linq.Expressions.Expression`1::Compile", "System.CodeDom.Compiler.", "Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.",
      // hot reload: replaces the IL of a loaded assembly's methods
      "System.Reflection.Metadata.MetadataUpdater::ApplyUpdate"
    ],
    // Rewriting a game method's instructions with a Harmony transpiler: the instructions Harmony
    // compiles are the game method's own and the ones the transpiler makes, whose operands are
    // reflection sinks in the analyzer. The labels and locals a transpiler asks its ILGenerator for.
    "code-rewrite": [
      "HarmonyLib.CodeInstruction", "HarmonyLib.CodeMatch", "HarmonyLib.Transpilers",
      "System.Reflection.Emit.ILGenerator::DefineLabel", "System.Reflection.Emit.ILGenerator::DeclareLocal"
    ],
    "serialization-unsafe": [
      "System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary.", "System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Soap.",
      "System.Runtime.Serialization.NetDataContractSerializer", "System.Web.Script.Serialization.JavaScriptSerializer"
    ],
    // The dynamic keyword and VB late binding resolve members by name at run time.
    "late-binding": [
      "Microsoft.CSharp.RuntimeBinder.", "Microsoft.VisualBasic.CompilerServices.NewLateBinding", "Microsoft.VisualBasic.CompilerServices.LateBinding",
      // dynamic expressions bind members by name at run time through a CallSiteBinder
      "System.Linq.Expressions.Expression::Dynamic", "System.Linq.Expressions.Expression::MakeDynamic", "System.Linq.Expressions.DynamicExpression"
    ],

    // Game modification
    "code-patching": ["HarmonyLib.", "MonoMod.", "Mono.Cecil."],

    // Recorded, no label
    "user-folders": ["System.Environment::GetFolderPath", "System.Environment/SpecialFolder"],
    "crypto": ["System.Security.Cryptography."],
    "compression": ["System.IO.Compression."],
    "threads": ["System.Threading.Thread::.ctor"],
    // Reading the bytes of the assembly's own embedded resources (see broadAccessCombinations).
    "resource-read": ["System.Reflection.Assembly::GetManifestResourceStream"],
    // Looking up and using types and members. Getting a type from a name, member access on a
    // resolved type, and whether what is invoked, created, read or written is traced to a name
    // are modelled by the analyzer itself; these rules only say the members are accounted for.
    // Longer rules above (Assembly::Load, Reflection.Emit) still apply.
    "reflection": [
      "System.Reflection.", "System.Type::", "System.Object::GetType", "System.Exception::GetType",
      "System.RuntimeTypeHandle::", "System.RuntimeMethodHandle::", "System.RuntimeFieldHandle::",
      "System.Activator::CreateInstance", "System.Delegate::", "System.MulticastDelegate::", "System.Attribute::",
      "System.AppDomain::GetAssemblies", "System.AppDomain::add_", "System.AppDomain::remove_",
      "System.Runtime.Loader.AssemblyLoadContext::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::GetUninitializedObject",
      "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::RunClassConstructor",
      // Expression trees: the factories that take a member or a type are reflection sinks in the
      // analyzer; compiling one is code generation unless the tree is built only from traced
      // members (see above); dynamic expressions are late binding.
      "System.Linq.Expressions."
    ],
    // An XmlSerializer for a type: Deserialize creates objects of that type and of the types its
    // public fields and properties (and its XmlInclude attributes) name, and sets their members;
    // Serialize reads them. The check does not follow which types those are (see "deep-game-access").
    // The other constructors and factories take more types, mappings or a location and stay
    // unclassified.
    "xml-serialization": [
      "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::.ctor(System.Type)", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::.ctor(System.Type,System.String)",
      "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::.ctor(System.Type,System.Xml.Serialization.XmlRootAttribute)"
    ],
    // The same with overrides, which can name more types (XmlArrayItemAttribute(string, Type), ...) that
    // the check does not follow: always "xml-serialization-open", whatever the first type is.
    "xml-serialization-open": [
      "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::.ctor(System.Type,System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAttributeOverrides)"
    ]
  },

  // Broad access: any of these capabilities or findings, in three groups. A mod is Broad access
  // when any reason in any group applies; the groups say why, for the player. Every group is
  // listed whatever the others hold. The name and summary of a group and the text of each reason
  // are shown to the user. A reason states what the check saw in the mod's files, never what the
  // mod intends.
  "broadAccess": {
    // Blind spots bounded to the game's or the mod's own code and memory, of the kind ordinary
    // modding has (policy 9: "unverified"). Nothing seen reaches outside the game, but the check
    // cannot rule it out, and the labels are what it saw, not a complete list. Not a safety claim:
    // an author who knows the rules can aim for this group alone. A blind spot moves here only with
    // a reason recorded in the changelog above.
    "deep-game-access": {
      "name": "Deep game access",
      "summary": "Works with the game's code and memory directly, in ways the check can't follow all the way.",
      "reasons": {
        "raw-memory": "Reads or writes memory directly",
        // Reflection the check traces only to a game or mod type and the types derived from it, or
        // to any type of a game or mod assembly (see the v10 changelog). Names built at run time on
        // other types, types from framework enumeration or from an object of an unknown type are
        // "unresolved-reflection" (system access).
        "bounded-reflection": "Uses reflection on the game's or its own code that the check cannot fully follow",
        // A Harmony transpiler: the game method Harmony compiles from the instructions it returns is
        // not one the check has read. What those instructions name is traced (an untraced operand is
        // "unresolved-reflection", OpCodes.Calli native code), but not what the game's own
        // instructions do once rearranged (see the v11 changelog). Bounded to the game's code only: a
        // transpiler on any other target is "rewrite-outside-game" (system access, v12).
        "code-rewrite": "Rewrites the game's code with a Harmony transpiler, which the check cannot follow instruction by instruction",
        // XmlSerializer runs the parameterless constructors and the setters of the types it fills
        // (and generates the code that does it, which is the framework's own); which types those are
        // is not followed. Its type is chosen in the mod's code, not by the XML it reads.
        "xml-serialization": "Reads or writes XML into objects whose types the check does not follow",
        // The same for a type the code does not fix where it makes the XmlSerializer: a generic
        // parameter (typeof(T)), a Type it is given, an object's run-time type (v15).
        "xml-serialization-open": "Reads or writes XML into objects of a type chosen at run time, which the check does not follow",
        // A game member that reaches the game's own XmlSerializer (see game.exempt), called where the
        // mod chooses the type it fills: a type argument that is not the game's, or a Type, object or
        // generic argument (and anything reflection or a reverse patch runs). Dropped at a call that
        // gives the game member only its own types or plain values (see v15).
        "xml-serialization-game": "Has the game read or write XML into objects of types the mod chooses, which the check does not follow"
      }
    },
    // Code the check read that uses features that can reach anything on the computer: they reach
    // outside the game openly, or are shaped like hiding (names built at run time, code generated or
    // loaded at run time).
    "system-access": {
      "name": "System access",
      "summary": "Uses features that can reach anything on your computer.",
      "reasons": {
        "native-code": "Runs native code",
        "dynamic-load": "Loads code at run time",
        "code-generation": "Builds code at run time",
        "process-start": "Starts other programs",
        "serialization-unsafe": "Uses a deserializer that can create any type",
        "late-binding": "Calls members by name at run time",
        "unresolved-reflection": "Uses reflection the check cannot trace",
        "com-activation": "Starts a COM component",
        "native-binary": "Ships a native program or library",
        // A Harmony transpiler on anything but the game's code (see the v12 changelog and "code-rewrite").
        "rewrite-outside-game": "Rewrites code other than the game's with a Harmony transpiler"
      }
    },
    // Parts of the mod the check could not inspect, or could not place: what they do is not known,
    // so nothing here says the mod does anything. Often a packaging problem the author can fix.
    "not-fully-checked": {
      "name": "Couldn't be fully checked",
      "summary": "Part of the mod couldn't be inspected.",
      "reasons": {
        "decode-failure": "Contains code the check cannot read",
        "unreadable-file": "Contains a file the check cannot read",
        "unextracted-archive": "Contains an archive the check cannot open",
        "not-analyzed": "Lists a file that was not analyzed",
        "ships-script": "Ships a script or installer",
        "embedded-binary": "Contains an embedded program, library or archive",
        // The game loads a different file from the one the check read under that name.
        "name-collision": "Ships a file named like a part of .NET, the game or a trusted library",
        // A gap in the policy, not something the mod does (policy 9 placed it with raw memory).
        "unclassified-reference": "Uses a part of .NET the check does not classify",
        "unanalyzed-call": "Calls code that was not analyzed",
        // A call into an assembly named like the game's (game.assemblies) that the game build it is checked
        // against does not have at all (with a game profile): usually a mod built for another game version.
        "missing-game-code": "Calls game code that this game version does not have"
      }
    }
  },

  // Broad access when a mod has every listed capability together (label or informational
  // capabilities: a Broad-access one decides the tier alone), with the "broadAccess" group it is
  // reported in. The
  // KSA mod loader loads any DLL in a mod's folder that something references, and reads
  // mod.toml, *.deps.json and the game's mod manifest from the mod and game folders. A
  // mod that connects to the internet and writes there can install code
  // for the next launch without loading any itself. The check does not follow what is written,
  // so the pair counts, not proof that downloaded bytes reach the write. A write outside the
  // game counts too: its path was not traced to the game's folders, which does not show it
  // avoids them (a path built at run time, or a Documents path whose folder names are obfuscated).
  // An embedded binary the check recognizes is Broad access on its own; one it cannot recognize
  // (compressed with deflate or brotli, or encrypted) must be read from the resource and written
  // out, so reading its own resources and writing such files is Broad access too.
  "broadAccessCombinations": [
    { "id": "network-code-write", "group": "system-access", "text": "Connects to the internet and changes files where the game loads code from", "caps": ["network", "file-write-code"] },
    { "id": "network-other-write", "group": "system-access", "text": "Connects to the internet and changes files the check cannot place outside the game's code folders", "caps": ["network", "file-write-outside"] },
    { "id": "resource-code-write", "group": "not-fully-checked", "text": "Writes out its own embedded data where the game loads code from", "caps": ["resource-read", "file-write-code"] },
    { "id": "resource-other-write", "group": "not-fully-checked", "text": "Writes out its own embedded data to places the check cannot place outside the game's code folders", "caps": ["resource-read", "file-write-outside"] }
  ],

  // Labels, in display order. A label applies when the mod has any listed capability. The id is
  // what results are keyed by (stable across rewordings of the text); ids are unique.
  "labels": [
    { "id": "game-changes", "text": "Changes how the game works", "caps": ["code-patching"] },
    { "id": "internet", "text": "Connects to the internet", "caps": ["network"] },
    { "id": "game-saves", "text": "Changes game saves", "caps": ["file-saves"] },
    { "id": "game-folder-writes", "text": "Changes files in its own or the game's folders", "caps": ["file-write-code"] },
    { "id": "outside-writes", "text": "Changes files outside the game", "caps": ["file-write-outside"] },
    { "id": "outside-reads", "text": "Reads files outside the game", "caps": ["file-read-outside"] },
    { "id": "user-info", "text": "Reads information about you and your computer", "caps": ["user-info", "environment-read", "process-list", "registry-read"] },
    { "id": "system-settings", "text": "Changes system settings", "caps": ["registry-write", "environment-persist", "certificate-store"] },
    { "id": "closes-programs", "text": "Closes other programs", "caps": ["process-kill"] },
    { "id": "ipc", "text": "Communicates with other programs on your computer", "caps": ["ipc"] },
    { "id": "opens-websites", "text": "Opens websites in your browser", "caps": ["shell-open"] },
    { "id": "opens-folders", "text": "Opens its own or the game's folders", "caps": ["shell-open-folder"] },
    { "id": "clipboard", "text": "Uses your clipboard", "caps": ["clipboard"] },
    { "id": "devices", "text": "Uses connected devices", "caps": ["serial-port"] }
  ],

  // Members known to need no capability. Same pattern syntax as "capabilities"; the most
  // specific matching pattern wins, so a capability rule can carve an exception out of a
  // safe namespace and vice versa.
  //
  // A framework member (an assembly of the .NET shared framework) that no rule here or in
  // "capabilities" matches is "unclassified-reference": Broad access (not fully checked). So a prefix belongs here
  // only when everything under it is capability-free: no files, network, processes, registry,
  // native code, raw memory, environment, code loading or reflection. Pointer types in a
  // member's signature are checked separately and stay raw memory. Members of other assemblies
  // (the mod, the game, trusted libraries) are not checked against this list.
  //
  // Deliberately left unclassified (not fully checked until someone decides): System.AppContext::SetSwitch
  // and SetData (switch runtime safety features; fixtures/classification/unclassified relies on
  // it), Queryable (calls built from MethodInfos; System.Linq.Expressions is classified since v10), System.Runtime.Intrinsics
  // (LoadUnsafe/StoreUnsafe read and write through a reference; the feature checks below are safe), the
  // .NET 10 handle types GCHandle`1, PinnedGCHandle`1 and WeakGCHandle`1 (GCHandle itself is classified
  // since v29), TimeZoneInfo, DriveInfo::set_VolumeLabel
  // (renames a volume), Debugger, trace listeners, XmlResolver and its subclasses,
  // XmlTextReader/XmlTextWriter/XPathDocument (open paths and URLs), XmlSerializer constructors other
  // than (Type), (Type, string), (Type, XmlRootAttribute) and (Type, XmlAttributeOverrides) and its
  // factories (more types, mappings, a location), Process members not listed here, the rest of
  // Microsoft.VisualBasic.FileIO (FileSystem copies and deletes, SpecialDirectories).
  "safe": [
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::SizeOf", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetLastWin32Error",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetLastPInvokeError", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetLastSystemError",
    // layout queries and error codes: they touch no memory (exact signatures; other overloads stay raw memory)
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::OffsetOf(System.Type,System.String)",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::OffsetOf(System.String)",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe::SizeOf()", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe::IsNullRef(!!0&)",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::SetLastPInvokeError(System.Int32)",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::SetLastSystemError(System.Int32)",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetHRForLastWin32Error()",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetPInvokeErrorMessage(System.Int32)",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::ThrowExceptionForHR(System.Int32)",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.Marshal::GetExceptionForHR(System.Int32)",

    // base types, primitives and value types
    "System.Object::", "System.String::", "System.Char::", "System.Boolean::", "System.Byte::", "System.SByte::",
    "System.Int16::", "System.UInt16::", "System.Int32::", "System.UInt32::", "System.Int64::", "System.UInt64::",
    "System.Int128::", "System.UInt128::", "System.IntPtr::", "System.UIntPtr::", "System.Half::", "System.Single::",
    "System.Double::", "System.Decimal::", "System.Enum::", "System.ValueType::", "System.Nullable::", "System.Nullable`1::",
    "System.ValueTuple::", "System.ValueTuple`", "System.Tuple::", "System.Tuple`", "System.TupleExtensions::",
    "System.Guid::", "System.DateTime::", "System.DateTimeOffset::", "System.TimeSpan::", "System.DateOnly::",
    "System.TimeOnly::", "System.TimeProvider::", "System.Version::", "System.Index::", "System.Range::", "System.HashCode::",
    "System.Uri::", "System.UriBuilder::", "System.Random::", "System.Convert::", "System.BitConverter::", "System.Buffer::",
    "System.Math::", "System.MathF::", "System.Lazy`", "System.WeakReference::", "System.WeakReference`1::",
    "System.StringComparer::", "System.FormattableString::", "System.OperatingSystem::", "System.GC::",
    "System.EventArgs::", "System.Progress`1::", "System.Attribute::.ctor",
    "System.Delegate::Combine", "System.Delegate::Remove", "System.Delegate::op_",
    // the interfaces these types implement
    "System.IDisposable::", "System.IAsyncDisposable::", "System.IComparable::", "System.IComparable`1::",
    "System.IEquatable`1::", "System.IFormattable::", "System.ISpanFormattable::", "System.IConvertible::",
    "System.ICloneable::", "System.IProgress`1::", "System.IObservable`1::", "System.IObserver`1::",
    "System.IFormatProvider::", "System.ICustomFormatter::",
    // delegates: invoking one runs a method whose reference is classified where it was taken
    "System.Action::", "System.Action`", "System.Func`", "System.Predicate`1::", "System.Comparison`1::",
    "System.Converter`2::", "System.EventHandler::", "System.EventHandler`1::", "System.AsyncCallback::",
    "System.Threading.ThreadStart::", "System.Threading.ParameterizedThreadStart::", "System.Threading.WaitCallback::",
    "System.Threading.TimerCallback::", "System.Threading.SendOrPostCallback::",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ConditionalWeakTable`2/CreateValueCallback::",
    // spans, memory and buffers over managed memory (bounds-checked)
    "System.Span`1::", "System.ReadOnlySpan`1::", "System.Memory`1::", "System.ReadOnlyMemory`1::",
    "System.MemoryExtensions::", "System.ArraySegment`1::", "System.Array::", "System.Buffers.",
    // views of a collection's own storage; still bounds-checked
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.CollectionsMarshal::",
    // exceptions
    "System.Exception::", "System.SystemException::", "System.ApplicationException::", "System.AggregateException::",
    "System.ArgumentException::", "System.ArgumentNullException::", "System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException::",
    "System.InvalidOperationException::", "System.NotSupportedException::", "System.NotImplementedException::",
    "System.PlatformNotSupportedException::", "System.FormatException::", "System.InvalidCastException::",
    "System.IndexOutOfRangeException::", "System.NullReferenceException::", "System.ObjectDisposedException::",
    "System.OperationCanceledException::", "System.OverflowException::", "System.ArithmeticException::",
    "System.DivideByZeroException::", "System.TimeoutException::", "System.OutOfMemoryException::",
    "System.InvalidProgramException::", "System.MissingMemberException::", "System.MissingMethodException::",
    "System.MissingFieldException::", "System.TypeLoadException::", "System.DllNotFoundException::",
    "System.EntryPointNotFoundException::", "System.KeyNotFoundException::", "System.UnauthorizedAccessException::",
    "System.IO.IOException::", "System.IO.FileNotFoundException::", "System.IO.DirectoryNotFoundException::",
    "System.IO.EndOfStreamException::", "System.IO.InvalidDataException::", "System.IO.PathTooLongException::",
    "System.Runtime.ExceptionServices.ExceptionDispatchInfo::",
    // collections and LINQ to objects
    "System.Collections.", "System.Linq.Enumerable::", "System.Linq.IGrouping`2::", "System.Linq.ILookup`2::",
    "System.Linq.Lookup`2::", "System.Linq.IOrderedEnumerable`1::", "System.Linq.ParallelEnumerable::",
    // numbers and vectors (pointer overloads are caught by the signature check)
    "System.Numerics.",
    // text: strings, encodings, formatting, regular expressions, JSON
    "System.Text.", "System.Globalization.",
    // XML documents in memory; loading and saving by path are file rules above
    "System.Xml.Linq.", "System.Xml.XmlNode::", "System.Xml.XmlElement::", "System.Xml.XmlAttribute::",
    "System.Xml.XmlNodeList::", "System.Xml.XmlNamedNodeMap::", "System.Xml.XmlAttributeCollection::",
    "System.Xml.XmlNameTable::", "System.Xml.NameTable::", "System.Xml.XmlDocument::", "System.Xml.XmlReader::",
    "System.Xml.XmlReaderSettings::", "System.Xml.XmlWriter::", "System.Xml.XmlWriterSettings::",
    "System.Xml.XPath.XPathNavigator::", "System.Xml.XPath.XPathNodeIterator::", "System.Xml.XPath.XPathExpression::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAttributeOverrides::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAttributes::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlElementAttribute::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlElementAttributes::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlRootAttribute::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAttributeAttribute::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlArrayAttribute::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlArrayItemAttribute::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializerNamespaces::", "System.Security.SecurityElement::Escape",
    // the overrides' collections, and the callbacks and event data for unknown XML nodes (a delegate runs a
    // method classified where it was taken)
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlArrayItemAttributes::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAnyElementAttributes::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAttributeEventArgs::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlElementEventArgs::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlNodeEventArgs::", "System.Xml.Serialization.UnreferencedObjectEventArgs::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlAttributeEventHandler::", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlElementEventHandler::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlNodeEventHandler::", "System.Xml.Serialization.UnreferencedObjectEventHandler::",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlDeserializationEvents::",
    // reading and writing XML for the type the constructor was given (see "xml-serialization"). A
    // pre-generated <Assembly>.XmlSerializers.dll next to that type's assembly is loaded when it
    // exists: a file in the mod's folder is one of its files (analyzed), and writing one there is
    // "Changes files in its own or the game's folders", as for any DLL the loader would load.
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::Serialize", "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::Deserialize",
    "System.Xml.Serialization.XmlSerializer::CanDeserialize",
    // IP addresses and endpoints are values: parsing, formatting, comparing (connecting is a socket)
    "System.Net.IPAddress::", "System.Net.IPEndPoint::", "System.Net.EndPoint::",
    // instruction values a transpiler matches or emits (emitting needs ILGenerator, which is code
    // generation, or a Harmony transpiler, which is code-rewrite; OpCodes.Calli is native code)
    "System.Reflection.Emit.OpCode", "System.Reflection.Emit.Label", "System.Reflection.Emit.LocalBuilder",
    "System.Reflection.Emit.OperandType", "System.Reflection.Emit.FlowControl", "System.Reflection.Emit.StackBehaviour",
    // compiler support: string interpolation, async state machines, awaiters
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.FormattableStringFactory::",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Async", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.PoolingAsync",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.TaskAwaiter", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ValueTaskAwaiter",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ConfiguredTaskAwaitable", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ConfiguredValueTaskAwaitable",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ConfiguredCancelableAsyncEnumerable", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.YieldAwaitable",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IAsyncStateMachine::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.INotifyCompletion::",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ICriticalNotifyCompletion::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ConditionalWeakTable`2::",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.StrongBox`1::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.ITuple::",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeFeature::", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.SwitchExpressionException::",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::InitializeArray", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::CreateSpan",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::EnsureSufficientExecutionStack",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::TryEnsureSufficientExecutionStack",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::GetHashCode", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::Equals",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::GetSubArray",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::IsReferenceOrContainsReferences",
    "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RuntimeHelpers::PrepareMethod", "System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InlineArrayAttribute::",
    "System.Runtime.JitInfo::",
    // threads and tasks inside the process (Thread::.ctor is "threads")
    "System.Threading.Tasks.", "System.Threading.Channels.", "System.Threading.Thread::", "System.Threading.Interlocked::",
    "System.Threading.Monitor::", "System.Threading.Volatile::", "System.Threading.Lock::", "System.Threading.Lock/",
    "System.Threading.CancellationToken::", "System.Threading.CancellationTokenSource::",
    "System.Threading.CancellationTokenRegistration::", "System.Threading.SemaphoreSlim::",
    "System.Threading.ManualResetEventSlim::", "System.Threading.CountdownEvent::", "System.Threading.Barrier::",
    "System.Threading.SpinLock::", "System.Threading.SpinWait::", "System.Threading.ReaderWriterLockSlim::",
    "System.Threading.ThreadLocal`1::", "System.Threading.AsyncLocal`1::", "System.Threading.LazyInitializer::",
    "System.Threading.Timer::", "System.Threading.PeriodicTimer::", "System.Threading.ThreadPool::QueueUserWorkItem",
    "System.Threading.SynchronizationContext::", "System.Threading.ExecutionContext::",
    "System.Threading.AutoResetEvent::.ctor(System.Boolean)", "System.Threading.ManualResetEvent::.ctor(System.Boolean)",
    "System.Threading.EventWaitHandle::Set", "System.Threading.EventWaitHandle::Reset", "System.Threading.WaitHandle::Wait",
    "System.Threading.WaitHandle::Dispose", "System.Threading.WaitHandle::Close",
    // unnamed kernel objects are the process's own (the named forms are "ipc")
    "System.Threading.Mutex::.ctor()", "System.Threading.Mutex::.ctor(System.Boolean)", "System.Threading.Mutex::ReleaseMutex",
    "System.Threading.Semaphore::.ctor(System.Int32,System.Int32)", "System.Threading.Semaphore::Release",
    "System.Threading.EventWaitHandle::.ctor(System.Boolean,System.Threading.EventResetMode)",
    // pinning or keeping an object alive, and comparing handles (what acts on a handle's address is raw memory)
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::Alloc", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::get_IsAllocated",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::Equals", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::GetHashCode",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::op_Equality", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.GCHandle::op_Inequality",
    // CPU feature checks: a constant per machine (the ISA classes the index uses; their loads and stores take pointers)
    "System.Runtime.Intrinsics.X86.Avx::get_IsSupported$", "System.Runtime.Intrinsics.X86.Fma::get_IsSupported$",
    "System.Runtime.Intrinsics.X86.Ssse3::get_IsSupported$", "System.Runtime.Intrinsics.X86.Sse2/X64::get_IsSupported$",
    "System.Runtime.Intrinsics.Arm.AdvSimd::get_IsSupported$",
    // diagnostics inside the process
    "System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch::", "System.Diagnostics.Debug::", "System.Diagnostics.StackTrace::",
    "System.Diagnostics.StackFrame::", "System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis.",
    // configuring and waiting for a program started with Process.Start (which is the capability)
    "System.Diagnostics.ProcessStartInfo::", "System.Diagnostics.DataReceivedEventArgs::",
    "System.Diagnostics.DataReceivedEventHandler::", "System.Diagnostics.Process::.ctor", "System.Diagnostics.Process::set_StartInfo",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_StartInfo", "System.Diagnostics.Process::WaitForExit", "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_ExitCode",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_HasExited", "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_StandardInput",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_StandardOutput", "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_StandardError",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::BeginOutputReadLine", "System.Diagnostics.Process::BeginErrorReadLine",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::add_OutputDataReceived", "System.Diagnostics.Process::add_ErrorDataReceived",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::add_Exited", "System.Diagnostics.Process::set_EnableRaisingEvents",
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_Id", "System.Diagnostics.Process::Dispose", "System.Diagnostics.Process::Close",
    // the game's own process and when it started (what can be done with the object is each member's own rule)
    "System.Diagnostics.Process::GetCurrentProcess", "System.Diagnostics.Process::get_StartTime",
    "System.ComponentModel.Component::Dispose",
    // console output and input of the game process
    "System.Console::",
    // streams, readers and writers over data the mod already has; opening a file is a file rule above
    "System.IO.Stream::", "System.IO.MemoryStream::", "System.IO.TextReader::", "System.IO.TextWriter::",
    "System.IO.StringReader::", "System.IO.StringWriter::", "System.IO.BinaryReader::", "System.IO.BinaryWriter::",
    "System.IO.StreamReader::", "System.IO.StreamWriter::", "System.IO.BufferedStream::",
    // a CSV parser over what it was given (opening a path is a file read above)
    "Microsoft.VisualBasic.FileIO.TextFieldParser::", "Microsoft.VisualBasic.FileIO.FieldType",
    "Microsoft.VisualBasic.FileIO.MalformedLineException::",
    // paths are strings; GetTempPath is a path source ("temp")
    "System.IO.Path::",
    // runtime and platform facts of the game process
    "System.Environment::get_CurrentManagedThreadId", "System.Environment::get_TickCount", "System.Environment::get_NewLine",
    "System.Environment::get_ProcessorCount", "System.Environment::get_StackTrace", "System.Environment::get_Is64Bit",
    "System.Environment::get_Version", "System.Environment::get_ProcessId", "System.Environment::get_HasShutdownStarted",
    "System.Environment::get_OSVersion", "System.Environment::get_CurrentDirectory", "System.Environment::Exit",
    "System.Environment::FailFast", "System.Environment::GetCommandLineArgs", "System.Environment::get_ProcessPath",
    "System.AppDomain::get_CurrentDomain", "System.AppDomain::get_BaseDirectory", "System.AppDomain::get_FriendlyName",
    "System.AppContext::get_BaseDirectory", "System.AppContext::TryGetSwitch", "System.AppContext::GetData",
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.RuntimeInformation::", "System.Runtime.InteropServices.OSPlatform::",
    // the callback type of NativeLibrary.SetDllImportResolver (which is native code)
    "System.Runtime.InteropServices.DllImportResolver::",
    // streams of a file opened by a file rule above
    "System.IO.FileStream::"
  ],

  // Recorded and reported in --facts output; no label, no effect on the tier.
  // "unsafe-code": the assembly is compiled with unsafe code enabled (the flag alone, see v27).
  "informational": ["user-folders", "crypto", "compression", "threads", "environment-process", "reflection", "resource-read", "unsafe-code"],

  // File access is labelled by where each path comes from. The path operand of each file
  // operation is traced through the values it is built from: the sources below, literals, the
  // method's arguments (from every analyzed call), fields (from every store) and the results of
  // the mod's methods. Paths from "inside" origins get no label; any other origin, or a part of the
  // path the check cannot trace, counts as outside the game. File capabilities reached through
  // reflection, a Harmony patch or a method taken as a delegate cannot be traced and count as
  // outside the game. A game method's file access arrives as the labels the game profile gives it
  // (traced there the same way; the game's own writes into its folders are left out).
  "fileAccess": {
    "kinds": { "file-write": "write", "file-delete": "write", "file-read": "read", "file-watch": "read" },
    "inside": ["own", "game", "saves"],
    // Inside origins the loader reads code or mod settings from: the mod's own folder, the game's
    // Content/<id>/ and Documents mods/<id>/ folders, mod.toml, *.deps.json and the game's mod
    // manifest. Writes there are "file-write-code" (labelled; see broadAccessCombinations). The
    // game's own writes there, seen through the game-API map, are not labelled.
    "code": ["own", "game"],
    // Member -> origin of the path it returns. "special-folder" is Documents for the folder
    // "documentsFolder", any other user folder otherwise. The Documents folder itself followed
    // directly by text matching "gameFolder" (separators made explicit: Path.Combine and Path.Join
    // operands are joined with "/") is the game folder; anything else in Documents, and anything
    // under an environment variable, is outside. A literal matching "absolutePathLiteral" (rooted on
    // any platform, or a URI) that starts a path is an absolute path; a ".." segment in a literal,
    // or one that parts joined end to end can make, is untraced.
    "sources": {
      "System.Reflection.Assembly::get_Location": "own",
      "KSA.Mod::get_DirectoryPath": "own",
      "KSA.FileReference::get_ModPath": "own",
      "System.AppContext::get_BaseDirectory": "game",
      "System.AppDomain::get_BaseDirectory": "game",
      "System.IO.Path::GetTempPath": "temp",
      "KSA.Constants::get_DocumentsFolderPath": "game",
      "KSA.ModLibrary::get_LocalModsFolderPath": "game",
      "KSA.ModLibrary::get_LocalManifestPath": "game",
      "KSA.GameSaves::get_SaveFolderPath": "saves",
      "System.Environment::GetFolderPath": "special-folder",
      "System.Environment::GetEnvironmentVariable": "environment"
    },
    // File operations that also take a web address in place of a path, and download it: one whose
    // path is a web address, or whose beginning cannot be traced (so could be one), connects to the
    // internet. A path that begins with a folder the check traced is a file, whatever is appended.
    "urls": [
      "System.Xml.XmlDocument::Load(System.String)", "System.Xml.XmlReader::Create(System.String,...)",
      "System.Xml.Linq.XDocument::Load(System.String,...)", "System.Xml.Linq.XElement::Load(System.String,...)"
    ],
    "documentsFolder": 5,
    "gameFolder": "^[\\\\/]+My Games[\\\\/]+Kitten Space Agency([\\\\/]|$)",
    "absolutePathLiteral": "^([A-Za-z]:|[\\\\/]|file:)"
  },

  // Exemptions (in "game" and "libraries" below). A game or trusted-library member passes every
  // capability it reaches to the mods that call it, Broad-access ones included (libraries also
  // pass findings such as untraced reflection). An exemption stops the listed capabilities at
  // the matching members while the game or library profile is built: they are dropped where
  // they are found in such a member and do not flow from it to its callers or to a mod. Use it
  // for a Broad-access capability that is the member's intended job and is accounted for another
  // way, or for a capability the check over-approximates there (a label capability only then).
  // "as" passes a label capability (or a Broad-access capability that the mod's call narrows) instead.
  // "atCall": the exempted capabilities are accounted for at the mod's direct call (its arguments
  // are traced there), so the member taken as a delegate or method handle, or reached by
  // reflection or a patch, is "unresolved-reflection" at the mod. "member" uses the capability pattern syntax without
  // a parameter list or "#N" (profiles merge overloads); "members" lists several patterns that share
  // the capabilities and the reason. Every exemption needs a reason. Profiles
  // record the policy's SHA-256, so they must be regenerated after any change here.

  // Game assemblies, used when no game profile is given. "Brutal.*" matches by prefix.
  "game": {
    "assemblies": ["KSA", "Brutal.*", "Planet.*"],
    // Game members only (member names and reasons; no game code). Curated against the game build
    // the index was run with (see README, "Known gaps"). Everything not listed passes on, for
    // example a game method that runs git with arguments its caller gives, or opens a folder.
    "exempt": [
      // The engine's own bindings (Vulkan, GLFW, Dear ImGui, ImPlot, FMOD, RakNet, shaderc, KTX,
      // stb), its math and memory types, the physics engine and the libraries the game ships use
      // P/Invoke, function pointers, Unsafe and MemoryMarshal throughout; almost every game member
      // reaches them. A mod calling the game API does not choose what that native code runs or
      // which memory it touches. Pointer types in a called member's signature are still flagged at
      // the mod's call. Listed by namespace, so a library a game update adds is not covered.
      { "member": "Brutal.", "caps": ["native-code", "raw-memory"], "reason": "game engine bindings, math and memory types" },
      { "member": "KSA.", "caps": ["native-code", "raw-memory"], "reason": "game code calling its engine and math types" },
      { "member": "Core.", "caps": ["native-code", "raw-memory"], "reason": "game renderer (Planet.Core)" },
      { "member": "Render.", "caps": ["native-code", "raw-memory"], "reason": "game renderer (Planet.Render.Core)" },
      { "member": "RenderCore.", "caps": ["native-code", "raw-memory"], "reason": "game renderer and shader compiler binding" },
      { "member": "BepuPhysics.", "caps": ["native-code", "raw-memory"], "reason": "physics engine shipped with the game" },
